/* GENERATED by knowledge/scripts/build-course-notes.mjs on 2026-10-21: Evelyn's answers, the general layer and her shelf, per lesson. Don't edit; rebuild. */
window.SCHOOL_NOTES = {"1-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Skin renews itself in roughly four weeks when young, slower with age (five to six weeks or more). A product or treatment that works through turnover (acids, retinoids, acne routines, pigment correction) needs at least one full cycle to show, and usually two or three. That's why consistent home care over 8 to 12 weeks matters more than any single visit, and why a routine shouldn't be judged in a week.","Deeper skin tones (more melanin) are more likely to leave a dark mark after any irritation, breakout or over-aggressive treatment (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation). Gentler acids such as mandelic and lactic, careful peel depth, no picking, and daily SPF matter even more. Lighter skin tends to go red rather than dark. Neither is \"better\"; the aftercare rules just weigh differently.","Pore size is set by genetics and oil flow. Pores can't be closed or shrunk, but they look smaller when they're kept clear of oil and dead skin (exfoliation, retinoids, clay or sulfur masks) and when the skin around them is firmer. Nothing \"tightens\" a pore for good."],"shelf":[]},"1-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Type is what the skin tends to be: oily, dry, combination (oily T-zone, drier cheeks) or normal. It's mostly genetic and changes slowly, with age and hormones.","Condition is what the skin is doing right now: dehydrated, sensitized, congested, breaking out, hyperpigmented. Conditions come and go, and products, weather, stress and habits move them.","Any type can have any condition. Oily skin can be dehydrated; dry skin can break out.","Dry skin makes less oil. It feels rough or flaky most of the time and usually wants richer moisturizers.","Dehydrated skin is short on water, not oil. It feels tight after cleansing, looks dull, shows fine lines that smooth out with hydration, and can be oily at the same time. Over-cleansing, over-exfoliating, heat, wind and dry air are common causes. Hydrating toners, humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid) and a moisturizer that seals them in usually help.","Pore size is set by genetics and oil flow. Pores can't be closed or shrunk, but they look smaller when they're kept clear of oil and dead skin (exfoliation, retinoids, clay or sulfur masks) and when the skin around them is firmer. Nothing \"tightens\" a pore for good."],"shelf":[]},"1-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["The outermost layer of skin, with its oils (lipids), keeps water in and irritants out. When it's worn down (too many actives, harsh cleansers, hot water, scrubbing, cold wind, sunburn), skin stings with products that used to be fine, looks red or shiny-tight, flakes, and breaks out more. A barrier recovers with fewer actives, a gentle cleanser, a moisturizer with ceramides or other barrier lipids, and SPF. Most \"my skin suddenly hates everything\" stories are a tired barrier.","Dry skin makes less oil. It feels rough or flaky most of the time and usually wants richer moisturizers.","Dehydrated skin is short on water, not oil. It feels tight after cleansing, looks dull, shows fine lines that smooth out with hydration, and can be oily at the same time. Over-cleansing, over-exfoliating, heat, wind and dry air are common causes. Hydrating toners, humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid) and a moisturizer that seals them in usually help.","A full routine isn't required to start. Cleanser, moisturizer and SPF are the base; one treatment product aimed at the main concern is the first addition. Adding everything at once makes a reaction impossible to trace.","Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated)."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-cran-peptide-cream","name":"Cran-Peptide Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"Rich and restorative. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-hydrabalance-gel","name":"HydraBalance Gel","brand":"Face Reality","price":34,"about":"Hydrates and balances oil. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-barrier-repair-cream","name":"Barrier Repair Cream","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":83.2,"about":"With amino acids. Part of the GlyMed Plus Elite line; step 3 of its routine: balance."},{"id":"gm-cell-protection-balm","name":"Cell Protection Balm","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":34,"about":"Step 3 of the GlyMed Plus routine: balance."}]},"2-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Gel and foaming cleansers lift oil well; usually for oily and combination skin, or a second cleanse.","Cream and milk cleansers are gentler; usually for dry, dehydrated or sensitized skin.","Cleansing oils and balms dissolve makeup and SPF; often the first step of a double cleanse at night.","Cleansing twice a day is enough. \"Squeaky clean\" usually means stripped."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-cream-cleanser","name":"Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":37,"about":"A daily calming wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-gentle-gel-cleanser","name":"Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser (travel size)","brand":"Face Reality","price":14,"about":"A daily antioxidant wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-mandelic-face-and-body","name":"L-Mandelic Face and Body Wash","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Cleanses and brightens. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-ultra-gentle-cleanser","name":"Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"A daily antioxidant wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-glycolic-cleanser","name":"Glycolic Facial Cleanser","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":41,"about":"With 10% glycolic acid. Step 1 of the GlyMed Plus routine: cleanse."},{"id":"gm-vitamin-c-cleanser","name":"Vitamin C Cleanser","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":47,"about":"Step 1 of the GlyMed Plus routine: cleanse."}]},"2-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated).","Peptides signal the skin to make more collagen and support firmness. Gentle; usually fine with everything."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-cran-peptide-cream","name":"Cran-Peptide Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"Rich and restorative. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-hydrabalance-gel","name":"HydraBalance Gel","brand":"Face Reality","price":34,"about":"Hydrates and balances oil. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-barrier-repair-cream","name":"Barrier Repair Cream","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":83.2,"about":"With amino acids. Part of the GlyMed Plus Elite line; step 3 of its routine: balance."},{"id":"gm-cell-protection-balm","name":"Cell Protection Balm","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":34,"about":"Step 3 of the GlyMed Plus routine: balance."}]},"2-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Mineral (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) sits on the skin and reflects and absorbs UV; usually the first choice for sensitive, acne-prone, post-treatment and pregnant clients. Can leave a cast on deeper tones; tinted versions help.","Chemical (avobenzone, octocrylene and others) absorb UV; lighter textures, more likely to sting after a treatment.","Either way: broad spectrum, SPF 30 or more, enough of it (about a quarter teaspoon for the face), reapplied every two hours in the sun. Makeup with SPF is a top-up, not the base.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-brilliant-defense-50","name":"Brilliant Defense SPF 50","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 50 sunscreen, with BrightShield Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-daily-spf-30","name":"Daily SPF 30 Plus","brand":"Face Reality","price":36,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 30 sunscreen. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-mineral-matte-spf-28","name":"Mineral Matte SPF 28","brand":"Face Reality","price":36,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 28 sunscreen. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-powdermineral-spf-50","name":"PowerMineral SPF 50","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 50 sunscreen, with CalmShield Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"mx-spf-50-mineral-spf","name":"Mineral SPF 50","brand":"Mixi Skincare","price":65,"about":"UVA/UVB protection. Medical-grade sunscreen."}]},"3-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Common triggers for stinging and redness on sensitized skin. \"Unscented\" can still contain masking fragrance; \"fragrance-free\" is the clearer label.","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"3-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["A full routine isn't required to start. Cleanser, moisturizer and SPF are the base; one treatment product aimed at the main concern is the first addition. Adding everything at once makes a reaction impossible to trace.","Products from different lines can usually sit in the same routine. What matters is the ingredient overlap: two exfoliants, two retinoids or several actives at once are the problem, not the brand mix. Whether a particular client should keep a particular product is a question for Evelyn.","A pea for a retinoid or acne medication across the whole face; a few drops of serum; a nickel of moisturizer; a quarter teaspoon of sunscreen for the face alone. More active isn't faster, it's just more irritating."],"shelf":[]},"3-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["A few skipped days don't reset progress. Pick the routine back up at the same strength; a week or more away from a retinoid or acid, go back to a few nights a week first.","Cleanser, moisturizer, sunscreen and the one treatment product the routine is built around; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so a hydrating mist and more moisturizer help, and sun exposure on vacation is the usual reason to go easier on actives for a week.","A 1 oz serum used once a day lasts about two to three months; a 2 oz moisturizer about two to three months; a 6 oz cleanser three to four months; a 2 oz sunscreen used properly on the face, about a month. Opened products are best used within six to twelve months; sunscreen and vitamin C lose strength faster when they're warm or in light."],"shelf":[]},"4-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["AHAs (alpha hydroxy acids) loosen dead cells on the surface and, over time, help texture, tone and dullness. Glycolic is the smallest and strongest; lactic is gentler and hydrating; mandelic is the largest and gentlest, works more slowly, and is a common choice for sensitive skin, deeper skin tones and acne because it's less likely to cause irritation and dark marks, and it has some antibacterial action.","BHA (salicylic acid) is oil-soluble, so it gets into pores: blackheads, congestion, oily skin. Usually 0.5 to 2% at home.","Enzymes (papaya, pumpkin, pineapple) digest dead surface cells without acid; the gentlest option, common in facials.","Scrubs (physical exfoliants) can work on body skin; on the face, harsh grains are a common cause of irritation and are usually skipped when acids or retinoids are in the routine.","Too much exfoliation looks like: shine with tightness, redness, stinging, more breakouts, flaking. Less is more; most skin does well with acids a few nights a week, not every night."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-11pct-mandelic-serum","name":"11% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-15pct-mandelic-serum","name":"15% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":48,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-5pct-mandelic-serum","name":"5% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-8pct-mandelic-serum","name":"8% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":44,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-glowtone-corrective","name":"GlowTone Corrective Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":64,"about":"Targets discoloration, with Hexa-Bright Complex."},{"id":"fr-hydraremedy-gel-serum","name":"HydraRemedy Gel Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Hydrates, repairs and soothes, with Tri-Recovery Complex. Acne-safe."}]},"4-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["One new product at a time, two weeks apart, so a reaction has a clear cause.","Patch test behind the ear or on the jaw for two or three nights before using an active on the whole face.","Actives start two or three nights a week and build up as the skin allows. A new sting or flaking means slow down, not push through.","Expect the first few weeks of a retinoid or acid to include some dryness or small breakouts; judge a routine at 8 to 12 weeks, not 8 to 12 days."],"shelf":[]},"4-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["A sterile blade gently removes the top layer of dead skin and the fine vellus hair (\"peach fuzz\"). Painless; skin is smooth and makeup sits better right away. Slight pinkness for a few hours is normal. Hair grows back the same (not thicker or darker; that's a myth), usually in three to five weeks. Commonly done every four to six weeks and often paired with a facial or a light peel, which then penetrates better. Not usually done on active breakouts, open skin or very reactive skin.","Skin is smooth and a little pink for a few hours. Makeup glides on the next day. No exfoliants for a few days; SPF daily, since the new surface burns easily. Hair returns as it was, in three to five weeks."],"shelf":[]},"5-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"5-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Retinol has to convert in the skin before it works; the gentlest, slowest, most widely used.","Retinal (retinaldehyde) is one step closer to the active form: faster results than retinol, a little more potential for irritation, still available without a prescription.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) are stronger and are prescribed and managed by a doctor; they also change what an esthetician can safely do (see red flags and scope).","All retinoids: start low and slow (two or three nights a week), buff over moisturizer if sensitive, expect some dryness or flaking in the first weeks, use SPF daily, and pause before exfoliating treatments and waxing. Not used in pregnancy or while breastfeeding without a doctor's say-so; most estheticians treat that as a hard stop."],"shelf":[]},"5-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Benzoyl peroxide kills acne bacteria and helps clear inflamed breakouts. It bleaches fabric (towels, pillowcases), can dry and irritate, and is usually started at a low strength (2.5% is often as effective as higher percentages with less irritation) for short contact or a few nights a week before building up.","Sulfur absorbs oil and calms inflamed spots; a common spot treatment and mask ingredient.","Salicylic acid and mandelic acid: see exfoliants.","Azelaic acid helps breakouts, redness and dark marks; often well tolerated.","Hydrocolloid patches draw fluid from a whitehead and stop picking; they do nothing for deep, closed bumps."],"shelf":[]},"6-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[]},"6-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["A retinoid and an acid (AHA/BHA) the same night. Alternate nights instead.","Two exfoliants at once (two acids, an acid plus a scrub, an acid plus an exfoliating cleanser every day). Pick one.","Benzoyl peroxide and retinol together can cancel each other and irritate; most routines use benzoyl peroxide in the morning and the retinoid at night, or alternate.","Pure vitamin C and an acid or retinoid in the same step: usually vitamin C in the morning, actives at night.","Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together; that caution is outdated."],"shelf":[]},"6-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Purging happens in the first two to six weeks after starting something that speeds turnover (acids, retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, an acne program): clogs that were already forming surface faster, in the places the client usually breaks out, as small whiteheads or bumps that clear quickly. It settles within about six weeks. A breakout from a product shows up in new places, with itching, redness or stinging, lasts longer, and keeps going. Purging is a reason to keep going (while easing frequency if it's harsh); a reaction is a reason to stop and message Evelyn.","One new product at a time, two weeks apart, so a reaction has a clear cause.","Patch test behind the ear or on the jaw for two or three nights before using an active on the whole face.","Actives start two or three nights a week and build up as the skin allows. A new sting or flaking means slow down, not push through.","Expect the first few weeks of a retinoid or acid to include some dryness or small breakouts; judge a routine at 8 to 12 weeks, not 8 to 12 days."],"shelf":[]},"7-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Cleanser, moisturizer, sunscreen and the one treatment product the routine is built around; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so a hydrating mist and more moisturizer help, and sun exposure on vacation is the usual reason to go easier on actives for a week.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","See skin basics. Dry skin wants richer creams and gentle cleansing; dehydrated skin wants humectants under a moisturizer, fewer foaming cleansers and acids, and a humidifier in dry rooms. Both want SPF."],"shelf":[]},"8-1":{"evelyn":["What do you ask a client first: what are your skin concerns and what does your current routine look like?"],"general":["Acne is pores clogged by oil and dead skin, with bacteria and inflammation on top. Non-inflamed acne is blackheads (open, oxidized) and whiteheads (closed); inflamed acne is red papules, pustules, and deeper nodules and cysts. Most acne routines combine: gentle cleansing twice a day, an exfoliant that keeps pores clear (mandelic or salicylic acid), an antibacterial (benzoyl peroxide, sulfur), hydration that doesn't clog (gel moisturizers), SPF, and nothing in the routine that feeds it (heavy oils, comedogenic makeup, hair products on the hairline, dirty pillowcases, phones). It takes 8 to 12 weeks to judge, and consistency beats intensity. Cystic, scarring or painful acne, acne that hasn't moved after months of consistent care, or acne that's clearly hormonal usually needs a doctor alongside the esthetician.","Oil production rises with androgens, which is why breakouts cluster at puberty, around periods, with some birth control changes, with PCOS and in stressful stretches. Jawline and chin breakouts that come and go monthly are classic. Routines help, but when the driver is hormonal, a doctor is often part of the answer. That's a hand-off, not a product.","Blackheads are oxidized oil in open pores; sebaceous filaments (the grey dots on the nose that come back in weeks) are normal and can only be minimized. Salicylic acid, retinoids, clay, and professional extractions clear congestion; pore strips and squeezing stretch pores and leave marks."],"shelf":[]},"8-2":{"evelyn":["Morning: what do you recommend from your shelf: for morning routine with dry/normal skin clients look like barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, 5%-8% (depends on clients tolorance), cran peptide, eye gel and mineral spf or brilliant defense for oily/combo/normal it usually looks like ultra gentle gel cleanser, moisture balance, eye gel, 8-15% mandelic (depends on tolerance), barrier gel cream or mineral spf or brilliant sometimes its mixed around really varies","Her picks: Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser, Soothing Radiance Toner, 8% L-Mandelic Serum, 5% L-Mandelic Serum, 15% L-Mandelic Serum, 11% L-Mandelic Serum, Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser, HydraRemedy Gel Serum and 8 more.","What should they avoid: do not over exfoliate avoid using actives without being given aftercare instuctions first, always follow aftercare instructions and if anything is burning, stinging or sensitive reach out immediately to make changes. If skin is experiencing Eczema,dermatitis, Psoriasis or any unusual reactions/flare ups stop actives immediately (list examples of active ingredients for them so they are aware of what to stop)"],"general":["Benzoyl peroxide kills acne bacteria and helps clear inflamed breakouts. It bleaches fabric (towels, pillowcases), can dry and irritate, and is usually started at a low strength (2.5% is often as effective as higher percentages with less irritation) for short contact or a few nights a week before building up.","Sulfur absorbs oil and calms inflamed spots; a common spot treatment and mask ingredient.","Salicylic acid and mandelic acid: see exfoliants.","Azelaic acid helps breakouts, redness and dark marks; often well tolerated.","Hydrocolloid patches draw fluid from a whitehead and stop picking; they do nothing for deep, closed bumps."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-2-5pct-adv-acne-med","name":"2.5% Advanced Acne Med","brand":"Face Reality","price":29,"about":"Benzoyl peroxide acne medication."},{"id":"fr-5pct-adv-acne-med","name":"5% Advanced Acne Med","brand":"Face Reality","price":30,"about":"Benzoyl peroxide acne medication."},{"id":"fr-invisiclear","name":"invisiClear Hydrocolloid Spot Patches","brand":"Face Reality","price":10,"about":"Professional-grade patches that visibly flatten and drain breakouts. They cover and protect, they're translucent, and they suit all skin types."},{"id":"fr-sulfur-spot-treatment","name":"Sulfur Spot Treatment","brand":"Face Reality","price":35,"about":"A 6% sulfur acne treatment."},{"id":"mx-hydroxi","name":"Hydroxi","brand":"Mixi Skincare","price":40,"about":"Hydrogen peroxide acne cream."}]},"8-3":{"evelyn":["How long until they see results: 3 to 6 months i tell everyone give it 90 days to start seeing real results but it varies by severity of skin concerns, consistency, and proper home care and following it correctly"],"general":["Deep, tender bumps along the jawline, chin and neck that flare before periods, with birth control changes, with PCOS or stress. Topicals help the surface (mandelic, benzoyl peroxide, spot treatments, consistent routine), but the driver is internal, so a doctor (dermatologist, OB-GYN or endocrinologist) is often part of clearing it. Supplements and medications for it are not the chat's to suggest.","The same acne, with sweat, friction and hair products added. Shower right after sweating, a salicylic or benzoyl peroxide body wash (left on a minute before rinsing), clean towels and sheets, no heavy conditioners running down the back, breathable fabrics. Body skin is tougher, so stronger exfoliants are tolerated, but still introduced slowly.","Supplements are outside an esthetician's lane. Whether to take, stop, mix or dose one is a question for the client's doctor or pharmacist, and for Evelyn to hear about, not for the chat to answer."],"shelf":[]},"9-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Flat brown or red marks left after a breakout or irritation (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation or erythema). They fade on their own over months; faster with daily SPF, gentle exfoliation, brighteners (niacinamide, vitamin C, azelaic, tranexamic, arbutin, mandelic acid), and no picking. Deeper tones mark more easily and keep marks longer. Pitted or raised scars are texture, not pigment, and need collagen-building treatments.","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call.","Deeper skin tones (more melanin) are more likely to leave a dark mark after any irritation, breakout or over-aggressive treatment (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation). Gentler acids such as mandelic and lactic, careful peel depth, no picking, and daily SPF matter even more. Lighter skin tends to go red rather than dark. Neither is \"better\"; the aftercare rules just weigh differently."],"shelf":[]},"9-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"9-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Sun spots are small, defined brown spots from cumulative sun. Melasma is larger, symmetrical, blotchy patches (cheeks, upper lip, forehead) driven by hormones (pregnancy, birth control) and sun, and it worsens with heat as well as UV. Both need relentless SPF, hats, and brighteners; melasma is stubborn, comes back with sun or heat, and is usually managed together with a doctor; aggressive peels and heat-based treatments can make it worse. Any new or changing dark patch should be seen by a professional.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","Mineral (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) sits on the skin and reflects and absorbs UV; usually the first choice for sensitive, acne-prone, post-treatment and pregnant clients. Can leave a cast on deeper tones; tinted versions help.","Chemical (avobenzone, octocrylene and others) absorb UV; lighter textures, more likely to sting after a treatment.","Either way: broad spectrum, SPF 30 or more, enough of it (about a quarter teaspoon for the face), reapplied every two hours in the sun. Makeup with SPF is a top-up, not the base."],"shelf":[]},"10-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Lines come from sun, expression, dryness and collagen loss with age. Daily SPF prevents most of it; retinoids, peptides and vitamin C support collagen; hydration softens lines that are really dehydration; professional collagen-inducing treatments (microchanneling, peels, oxygen and tightening facials) build on that. Results are gradual, over months, and are kept with maintenance.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","Tiny, controlled micro-injuries in the skin trigger it to make new collagen and elastin, and let serums (often growth factors) absorb. Used for acne scars, texture, pores, fine lines and uneven tone. Microchanneling devices stamp straight down; microneedling pens drag and are generally deeper and more medical. Expect redness like a sunburn for a day or two, tightness, sometimes light flaking, and no makeup for about a day. Results build over weeks as collagen forms, and a series (commonly four to six sessions, four weeks apart) is where the change shows. Not done on active acne, open skin, cold sores, during Accutane, or on skin that scars with keloids without a doctor's OK."],"shelf":[]},"10-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Retinol has to convert in the skin before it works; the gentlest, slowest, most widely used.","Retinal (retinaldehyde) is one step closer to the active form: faster results than retinol, a little more potential for irritation, still available without a prescription.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) are stronger and are prescribed and managed by a doctor; they also change what an esthetician can safely do (see red flags and scope).","All retinoids: start low and slow (two or three nights a week), buff over moisturizer if sensitive, expect some dryness or flaking in the first weeks, use SPF daily, and pause before exfoliating treatments and waxing. Not used in pregnancy or while breastfeeding without a doctor's say-so; most estheticians treat that as a hard stop.","Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated)."],"shelf":[]},"10-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["A cleanse (often double), exfoliation (enzyme or a gentle acid), steam or softening, extractions where needed, a mask aimed at the skin's condition, massage, and finishing products with SPF. Common additions: LED light, oxygen, high frequency, cold tools. A facial can't change skin in one visit; it clears, calms and resets, and the home routine does the work between visits.","How often: most estheticians suggest every four to six weeks (one skin cycle) for maintenance; every two to four weeks during an acne program or a corrective series.","Peels an esthetician performs are superficial: they work in the top layers to improve texture, tone, dark marks, fine lines and breakouts, and are repeated in a series (commonly three to six, two to four weeks apart). Deeper peels are medical.","A no-downtime peel (light, \"lunchtime\"): slight pinkness for an hour or two, maybe light flaking on day two or three, makeup the next day.","A medium-depth esthetic peel (stronger acids or layered): pink to red for a day or two, tightness, visible flaking or peeling on days two to five, and a week of gentle care. Not before a big event; plan a week.","Peels sting or feel warm while on the skin, usually for a few minutes, and are neutralized or self-neutralize. Some skin barely flakes and still benefits; peeling isn't the measure of a peel working.","Tiny, controlled micro-injuries in the skin trigger it to make new collagen and elastin, and let serums (often growth factors) absorb. Used for acne scars, texture, pores, fine lines and uneven tone. Microchanneling devices stamp straight down; microneedling pens drag and are generally deeper and more medical. Expect redness like a sunburn for a day or two, tightness, sometimes light flaking, and no makeup for about a day. Results build over weeks as collagen forms, and a series (commonly four to six sessions, four weeks apart) is where the change shows. Not done on active acne, open skin, cold sores, during Accutane, or on skin that scars with keloids without a doctor's OK.","Red light calms and supports collagen; blue light targets acne bacteria. Painless, no downtime, cumulative; a nice finisher."],"shelf":[]},"11-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Usually a thinned barrier: too many actives, harsh cleansing, hot water, fragrance, wind, sun. The fix is subtraction: a gentle fragrance-free cleanser, a moisturizer with barrier lipids, mineral SPF, and actives paused and reintroduced one at a time. Persistent flushing with bumps across the cheeks and nose, or redness that comes with heat, alcohol or spicy food, can be rosacea, which is a doctor's diagnosis.","Common triggers for stinging and redness on sensitized skin. \"Unscented\" can still contain masking fragrance; \"fragrance-free\" is the clearer label.","Fragrance, essential oils, nuts (in some oils), latex (gloves, some wax strips), lanolin, certain preservatives, and sulfites. A client who has reacted before is patch-tested or given a different product. Any new rash is a reason to stop the product and message Evelyn."],"shelf":[]},"11-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word.","Recent Botox or fillers: facials, massage and pressure on the area usually wait about two weeks; peels and dermaplaning, one to two weeks, per the injector."],"shelf":[]},"11-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Trouble breathing, or swelling of the lips, tongue, throat or around the eyes: emergency, 911.","A reaction that spreads, blisters, weeps, hives, or gets worse after the first day.","Signs of infection: increasing pain, heat, pus, red streaks, fever.","A mole or spot that is new, changing, asymmetrical, with an irregular border, more than one color, larger than a pencil eraser, or that bleeds or itches (the ABCDEs).","Cystic, scarring or painful acne; acne that hasn't responded to months of consistent care; suspected hormonal acne.","A licensed esthetician works on the surface of the skin to cleanse, exfoliate, improve and beautify: facials, superficial peels, dermaplaning, extractions, waxing, LED, skincare guidance and a home routine. An esthetician does not diagnose skin disease, treat medical conditions, prescribe or advise on medication, perform invasive or medical procedures, or promise results. When something looks medical, the answer is a referral to a doctor (a dermatologist for skin, the client's own doctor for medications and health). State boards set the exact limits; Evelyn knows hers."],"shelf":[]},"12-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word.","Recent Botox or fillers: facials, massage and pressure on the area usually wait about two weeks; peels and dermaplaning, one to two weeks, per the injector."],"shelf":[]},"12-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["The first 24 hours - Leave the skin alone: no makeup the rest of the day (mineral makeup is often allowed the next morning), no touching or picking. - No heat or sweat: skip workouts, saunas, steam rooms, hot yoga, hot showers on the face and swimming (chlorine and salt) for 24 to 48 hours. Sweat and heat on freshly exfoliated skin cause stinging and breakouts. - Cleanse gently that night or the next morning with a mild cleanser and lukewarm water; moisturize; SPF in the morning. - No actives: acids, retinoids, scrubs, benzoyl peroxide and exfoliating toners stay off for at least two or three days after a facial, five to seven days after a peel, dermaplaning or microchanneling, or until the skin has stopped flaking and feels normal. - Sun: avoid direct sun and tanning for a week, SPF every morning, reapplied outdoors.","The first week - Hydrate and protect: gentle cleanser, hydrating serum or mist, moisturizer, SPF. - Let flaking fall on its own. Peeling skin that's pulled off leaves dark marks or raw patches. - Change pillowcases and clean phone screens, especially after extractions. - Hold off on waxing, threading, dermaplaning at home, facial scrubs and new products for a week.","What's normal - Pink or slightly flushed skin for a few hours (longer after a peel). - Tightness and dryness for a day or two. - Light flaking on days two to five after a peel, dermaplaning or microchanneling. - A few small whiteheads or bumps in the day or two after extractions, as what was loosened surfaces; a short \"purge\" in acne-prone skin after a peel. - Mild tenderness where extractions were done.","What isn't normal, and means message Evelyn or see a doctor - Redness that spreads or gets worse after the first day instead of better. - Swelling, especially around the eyes or lips; hives; blistering; weeping or crusting. - Burning that doesn't settle in a day, or pain. - Pus, heat and spreading redness around a spot (possible infection), or fever. - A cold sore starting (lip tingling or blisters): a doctor can prescribe something fast. For breathing trouble or swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, call 911.","Peeling, if it comes, usually starts on day two or three and finishes by day five to seven. Don't pick or exfoliate it off. Keep the skin moisturized and out of the sun; a hat helps. Skin can look darker or blotchy before it flakes; that's the old surface lifting, not the result.","Red like a sunburn for 12 to 48 hours, warm and tight, sometimes pinpoint marks or light flaking by day three. Only the products given at the visit for the first day or two; no makeup for 24 hours; no sweat, heat or sun for 48 hours; no actives for about a week. Collagen builds for weeks, so the real result shows between sessions."],"shelf":[]},"12-3":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Exfoliated, extracted or waxed skin has a thinner barrier and open follicles for a day or two. Heat, sweat, friction, bacteria (hands, phones, pillowcases, gym equipment) and strong actives in that window are what turn a good treatment into redness, bumps or dark marks.","Trouble breathing, or swelling of the lips, tongue, throat or around the eyes: emergency, 911.","A reaction that spreads, blisters, weeps, hives, or gets worse after the first day.","Signs of infection: increasing pain, heat, pus, red streaks, fever.","A mole or spot that is new, changing, asymmetrical, with an irregular border, more than one color, larger than a pencil eraser, or that bleeds or itches (the ABCDEs).","Cystic, scarring or painful acne; acne that hasn't responded to months of consistent care; suspected hormonal acne."],"shelf":[]},"13-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Hair length: about a quarter inch (6 mm), the length of a grain of rice: roughly two to three weeks of growth after shaving, or four to six weeks after a wax. Too short and the wax can't grip; very long can be trimmed, but not shorter than that.","Don't shave between waxes. Shaving resets the growth cycle and makes the next wax patchier and more painful. Waxing gets easier with regular visits because the hair comes in finer and sparser.","Exfoliate gently two or three days before (not the day of), and moisturize in the days before so the skin isn't dry or tight.","Day of: clean, dry skin; no lotion, oil or deodorant on the area; loose, breathable clothing; some clients take an over-the-counter pain reliever 30 to 45 minutes before (their call, and their doctor's if they take other medicines).","Periods: waxing is possible during a period (a tampon or cup for a Brazilian); skin is often more sensitive in the days before and during, so some clients prefer to book around it.","Let the hair grow to the right length, exfoliate a couple of days before, shower that day, wear loose cotton, breathe out as each strip comes off, and know the first one is the most sensitive; it gets easier each time. It takes about 30 to 40 minutes. Tell the esthetician about any medications, skin issues or pregnancy first (pregnancy waxing is common but the skin is more sensitive; some clients clear it with their doctor)."],"shelf":[]},"13-2":{"evelyn":[],"general":["No heat or sweat: workouts, hot showers, baths, saunas, hot tubs, swimming.","No sun or tanning on the area.","No tight clothing on the waxed area; for a Brazilian, loose cotton underwear and no sex for about 24 hours.","No touching, no scented lotions, oils or deodorant on the area for the first day; a soothing, fragrance-free product or aloe is fine.","No exfoliating for 48 to 72 hours.","Starting two or three days after, exfoliate the area gently two or three times a week (a soft mitt or a gentle acid toner or pad; mandelic, lactic, glycolic or salicylic are common) and keep it moisturized. Ingrowns come from dead skin trapping new hair; exfoliation and no tight friction are the fix. Don't dig at an ingrown with tweezers; a warm compress and exfoliation let it surface, and a stubborn, painful or pus-filled one is a reason to message Evelyn or see a doctor.","Redness, warmth and small red bumps (a histamine response around the follicles) for a few hours to a day, longer for the first wax or on sensitive skin.","A few tiny white bumps a day or two later as follicles settle."],"shelf":[]},"14-1":{"evelyn":["When should they book a facial, and which one: all new clients who don't have acne concerns book - new clients start here all new clients who do have acne concerns book - acne program lumevina custom facial - this is for returning monthly clients - no acne concerns monthly acne treatment - this is for returning clients w/ acne concerns bcs this includes more time for acne peels and extractions bi weekly acne treatment - is for returning acne clients who are on my acne program and would benefit because they have severe acne ageless grace","Her picks: New Clients Start Here, Acne Bootcamp (New Client Consultation + Treatment)."],"general":["A look at the skin, a review of the current routine and habits, and a plan: which treatment, which products and in what order. Virtual consultations work for routine questions; in-person is better when the skin needs to be seen up close.","Clearing acne is a process, not a facial: a treatment every two to four weeks (exfoliation, extractions, calming and antibacterial steps, often a peel or LED) plus a strict, slowly escalated home routine. Most programs count on 90 days to see a real difference and three to six months to be mostly clear, with consistency at home as the deciding factor. Purging in the first weeks is common.","A cleanse (often double), exfoliation (enzyme or a gentle acid), steam or softening, extractions where needed, a mask aimed at the skin's condition, massage, and finishing products with SPF. Common additions: LED light, oxygen, high frequency, cold tools. A facial can't change skin in one visit; it clears, calms and resets, and the home routine does the work between visits.","How often: most estheticians suggest every four to six weeks (one skin cycle) for maintenance; every two to four weeks during an acne program or a corrective series.","These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word."],"shelf":[]},"14-2":{"evelyn":["Morning: what do you recommend from your shelf: for morning routine with dry/normal skin clients look like barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, 5%-8% (depends on clients tolorance), cran peptide, eye gel and mineral spf or brilliant defense for oily/combo/normal it usually looks like ultra gentle gel cleanser, moisture balance, eye gel, 8-15% mandelic (depends on tolerance), barrier gel cream or mineral spf or brilliant sometimes its mixed around really varies","Her picks: Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser, Soothing Radiance Toner, 8% L-Mandelic Serum, 5% L-Mandelic Serum, 15% L-Mandelic Serum, 11% L-Mandelic Serum, Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser, HydraRemedy Gel Serum and 8 more."],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-11pct-mandelic-serum","name":"11% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-15pct-mandelic-serum","name":"15% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":48,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-5pct-mandelic-serum","name":"5% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-8pct-mandelic-serum","name":"8% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":44,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-barrier-cream-cleanser","name":"Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":37,"about":"A daily calming wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."}]}};
